  {
    "episode_id": 96238,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/6/96238.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 1,
    "turn_text": "Well, Heidi, first let me say it's wonderful to be with you. And I'll tell you what I miss you on the Agriculture Committee and all of your, I'll talk about somebody who knows what they're talking about in rural America and agriculture. Your voice has been and continues to be really, really important. So thank you for that. And as you know, and of course I grew up in Northern Michigan small town rural community. As you know, what we are seeing that that certainly relates to what's happening on the climate crisis is that farmers who are You know are and have been the front line in terms of protecting our water and our land. I mean the first environmentalist I mean they they have an awful lot at stake, you know, at preserving our land and water and air. And now they are bearing the brunt of the climate crisis. And just with the weather fluctuations, I know in Michigan, we literally, beginning of the summer, huge droughts, and then it went to flooding and we have an awful lot of fruits and vegetables and our cherry orchards and apples and and peaches and so on you know they it warms up too early the buds come out on the trees then it freezes again and it kills everything and so you know they they're at the front of the spear here and so I've over the years, more and more heard from farmers saying, look, we get it. We know that there's a climate crisis. We get what's happening in the weather, but we're not sure what our role should be, to be able to help with this. And first, I tell them, already the efforts that you do on conservation are already helping to lead on keeping carbon in the ground and more planting trees. We're keeping it in the trees. And so the whole effort has been how do we support them through voluntary producer-led efforts to do more of what they know how to do and want to do. And there's new technology, new research going on. We know with, for instance, our dairy farmers and what they can do with methane digesters and managing manure in a way where you can actually create your own electricity, but keep the methane out of the air, which is even more dangerous then carbon when it comes to polluting the atmosphere. So,",
