  {
    "episode_id": 96238,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/6/96238.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 5,
    "turn_text": "Absolutely. And, you know, It's always, you know, there are a lot of people involved in this and appreciate your leadership efforts in the Ag community and the production, you know, all the commodity groups and so on. But I have to give a shout out to Senator Mike Braun, who is my Republican partner in this. He's from Indiana. I'm from Michigan. We get the Midwest and understand what's happening actually in the Great Lakes all around us. But so, Mike and I were able to come together and then build over time. It wasn't easy in the beginning, but build over time the coalition that we needed. And it's because we started with this basic premise. Look, we're not talking about regulations. We're talking about supporting farmers to do more of what they already do and to work with them to give them information and research and technology that will help them be able to sequester more carbon. We call it climate smart agriculture and research. So much more talking about are the kinds of programs that are already being done, but doing more cover crops, doing more buffer strips, doing more planting of trees, doing helping create financial opportunities around digesters and other things, helping through REAP for farmers to put solar and wind on the farm and use other kinds of technologies. We basically got this done by saying, look, we want to set up a pathway through an agency you trust, the USDA, not EPA, USDA, a voluntary effort. We're going to have an advisory committee primarily made up of producers. And then we want to create a system where Farmers may wish to continue to do more around climate-smart conservation, which is great, but if they want to take the next step and say, I want to measure the new things I'm doing and how much carbon I'm getting out of the atmosphere, keeping it in the ground, and I want some I want people with expertise that can tell me that I can count on the way we're going to measure it and that they can sort of give us the good seal of approval here from the USDA that in fact they are creating more carbon sequestration and then give them an opportunity to go in the private market and to sell carbon credits and actually have a revenue stream, make money. And we want to do this in a way where there's not a middleman from Wall Street. It's all about how do we write it for farmers and foresters and help them create additional revenue from doing the right thing. But have it with integrity, with experts that are going to be able to tell them how it should be done or measure it in a way so that they know they can count on the results. So that's what we're talking about.",
